```
@id("read-only-lookups")
permit (
  principal,
  action in [
    Drupe::Action::"authority_get_approval",
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder",
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_tasks",
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_costs",
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_actuals_vs_planned",
    Drupe::Action::"get_workorder_kpis",
    Drupe::Action::"get_schedule_calendar",
    Drupe::Action::"get_my_assigned_workorders",
    Drupe::Action::"get_failure_codes",
    Drupe::Action::"list_workorders"
  ],
  resource
);

@id("close-at-main-after-recent-human-approval")
permit (principal, action == Drupe::Action::"close_workorder", resource)
when { context.input.site_id == "MAIN" }
when temporal {
  formerly within 1h Drupe::Action::"authority_get_approval"::response{
    eventResource: resource,
    input.wonum: context.input.wonum,
    input.site_id: context.input.site_id,
    output.decision: "GRANTED",
    output.approver_kind: "HUMAN"
  }
};

@id("agent-never-approves")
forbid (principal, action == Drupe::Action::"approve_workorder", resource);
```

I assumed the `authority_get_approval` strings are `"GRANTED"` for `decision` and `"HUMAN"` for `approver_kind`. The manifest declares both as plain strings, so I took the vocabulary from `get_workorder`'s `approval_outcome` and `approver_type` enums. If the authority server returns different values, change those two literals.

Approval evidence comes only from the authority server's recorded response, which means the call succeeded. Denied or failed calls are recorded as errors and never match. Nothing outside the read-only lookups and the MAIN-site close is permitted, so `approve_workorder`, `update_workorder`, `assign_technician`, `cancel_workorder` and `generate_work_order` are all denied by default.
